  <section class="cho-section cho-history">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">A little history</span>
      <h2 class="disp">The map that broke a pandemic — and a bad idea.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="cho-history-grid">
      <article>
        <h3 class="disp">The miasma theory, disproven by a map.</h3>
        <p>In 1854, most doctors believed cholera spread through "bad air." John Snow didn't. During a lethal outbreak in London's Soho district, he interviewed residents and mapped every death by address, tracing the cluster to a single water pump on Broad Street. He had the pump handle removed; the outbreak was already fading, but his map — dots of death clustered tightly around a well — became one of the founding acts of modern epidemiology, decades before germ theory was widely accepted.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3 class="disp">The germ, found twice.</h3>
        <p>Italian anatomist Filippo Pacini identified the cholera bacterium under a microscope that same year, 1854 — and was ignored for decades. Robert Koch is far more commonly credited with discovering it, in 1883, during an outbreak in Egypt. The bacterium wasn't formally renamed to credit Pacini's overlooked work until 1965, more than a century after he first saw it.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="cho-timeline-outer">
      <button type="button" class="tl-arrow prev" id="tlPrev" aria-label="Previous milestone">‹</button>
      <div class="cho-timeline" id="choTimeline" aria-label="Cholera milestones" tabindex="0">
        <div class="tl-sp" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <div class="tl-ev"><div class="yr">1817</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">The first pandemic</b><p>Originating in the Ganges Delta, cholera spreads beyond South Asia for the first time along trade and military routes.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev"><div class="yr">1829–51</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">Crossing oceans</b><p>The second pandemic reaches Britain, then crosses the Atlantic to the Americas.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev ok"><div class="yr">1854</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">Snow's map</b><p>John Snow traces a Soho outbreak to the Broad Street pump, founding modern epidemiology.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev"><div class="yr">1854</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">Pacini sees it first</b><p>Filippo Pacini identifies <em>Vibrio cholerae</em> under a microscope the same year — and is ignored for decades.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev"><div class="yr">1883</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">Koch's rediscovery</b><p>Robert Koch isolates the cholera bacterium in Egypt, receiving the credit that outlives Pacini's earlier, overlooked work.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev sev"><div class="yr">1961</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">The seventh pandemic begins</b><p>A new strain, El Tor, emerges in Indonesia and spreads globally — the pandemic that, unlike the six before it, has never ended.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev ok"><div class="yr">1965</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">Pacini's name restored</b><p>The bacterium is formally renamed <em>Vibrio cholerae</em> Pacini 1854, honoring the scientist ignored for over a century.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev ok"><div class="yr">1971</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">Oral rehydration therapy</b><p>Researchers in Bangladesh and India prove a simple sugar-salt-water solution can replace intravenous fluids for most patients — cutting treatment cost by orders of magnitude.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev sev"><div class="yr">2010</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">Haiti</b><p>A country free of cholera for a century sees an outbreak begin near a UN peacekeeping base, ten months after a devastating earthquake.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev sev"><div class="yr">2016–21</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">Yemen's outbreak</b><p>A war-shattered water and health system produces the largest cholera outbreak in modern history — an estimated 2.5 million suspected cases.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev"><div class="yr">2017</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">A roadmap to zero</b><p>The Global Task Force on Cholera Control sets a goal to cut deaths 90% by 2030 through surveillance, vaccination, and water infrastructure.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev sev"><div class="yr">2022</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">Malawi's deadliest outbreak</b><p>The country records its worst cholera outbreak in two decades, worsened by cyclone flooding and a global vaccine shortage.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev"><div class="yr">2023</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">The vaccine runs short</b><p>Global demand for oral cholera vaccine outstrips supply so badly that the standard two-dose regimen is temporarily cut to one dose worldwide.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev"><div class="yr">Ongoing</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">Pandemic seven, still counting</b><p>More than sixty years on, the seventh pandemic has never been declared over.</p></div></div>
