  <section class="cho-section cho-ladder-section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">The cool diagram</span>
      <h2 class="disp">The race against dehydration.</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="cho-sub">Cholera doesn't damage the body directly so much as drain it — a patient can lose more than a tenth of their body weight in fluid within hours. Left untreated, that climb in fluid loss tracks almost exactly with a climb in fatality. Treated in time, that same climb barely matters. Hover a step to read it.</p>
    <div class="cho-ladder" id="choLadder"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="cho-section cho-spotlight">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Introduced, not inherited · Haiti, 2010</span>
      <h2 class="disp">The outbreak a country didn't have until peacekeepers arrived.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="cho-spotlight-grid">
      <article class="cho-spotlight-lead">
        <p>Haiti had been cholera-free for over a century before October 2010 — ten months after a devastating earthquake had already killed more than 200,000 people and left much of the country's infrastructure in ruins. A cluster of severe diarrheal illness appeared along the Artibonite River, near a base housing UN peacekeepers newly arrived from Nepal, where a cholera outbreak was active at the time. Genetic testing later matched the strain to a South Asian lineage nearly identical to Nepal's.</p>
        <p>Untreated sewage from the base had been discharged into a tributary that hundreds of thousands of Haitians used for drinking, bathing, and washing. The outbreak went on to infect roughly 820,000 people and kill nearly 10,000 over the following decade — a toll layered on top of the earthquake's own. The United Nations denied legal responsibility for years, citing diplomatic immunity, before its then Secretary-General issued a formal apology in 2016 — without an accompanying compensation fund for victims.</p>
      </article>
      <aside class="cho-spotlight-facts">
        <div class="cho-fact"><span class="n">~820,000</span><span class="k">Haitians infected in the decade following the outbreak's start</span></div>
        <div class="cho-fact"><span class="n">~10,000</span><span class="k">deaths, in a country that had recorded no cholera in living memory</span></div>
        <div class="cho-fact"><span class="n">6 yrs</span><span class="k">between the outbreak's start and the UN's formal apology, in 2016</span></div>
        <div class="cho-fact cho-fact-hope"><span class="n">2016</span><span class="k">Haiti finally receives a dedicated UN cholera response and elimination plan</span></div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cho-section cho-close">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">The reckoning</span>
      <h2 class="disp">Two centuries old. Cured for pennies. Never gone.</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="cho-close-line">Cholera isn't a mystery of science — it's been reliably curable since the 1970s with little more than clean water, sugar, and salt. What keeps it in its seventh pandemic isn't the bacterium; it's every collapsed sewage system, every under-supplied vaccine stockpile, and every government that treats clean water as optional. The map that founded epidemiology also proved the fix was never medical. It was <b>plumbing</b>.</p>
